## What is Quantization?

Quantization is the process of transforming the parameters of a deep learning model to use fewer bits per parameter representation. It’s a type of compression where the individual weight values are quantized for a model to fit discrete levels in a finite set according to the expected/desired range of values. So in place of a 32-bit floating point value, we’d use a 16-bit, 8-bit or 1-bit fixed integer to represent the weight value. The real benefit of quantization is the ability to run fast math on low precision values e.g. INT8 operations. This leads to faster performance with lower power consumption. Inference is mostly Matrix multiplications which are ideally suited to such fast low precision operations.

- Quantization can be performed on integer and floating-point values into multiple levels that can represent values with 1 to n-bits where n-bit = 16.
- Quantization helps to compress models which, improves model loading times and reduces the storage space needed.
- Quantization helps to perform operations at a lower precision (less bits) e.g. from 16-bit down to 8-bit or 4-bit, but can be as low as 1-bit, which improves the latency of running neural networks at lower precision with faster CPU/GPU hardware.
- FP16 is the standard precision “half precision” floating point rate used for several deep learning accelerators: NVIDIA (V100, T4 and T4) and AMD AI (Sofware). It is the default precision for PyTorch Mobile.

The compression with quantization is primarily to speedup the model performance, not to perform model compression. This will also speed up deep learning model inference performance, which is increasingly in demand for latency-sensitive applications such as those used on edge devices e.g. inference on mobile devices or other embedded devices. Indeed the differences between the performance of the original model and the quantized version become even more evident with these small devices with limited resources.

What kind of models could inference be performed on these small edged devices?

- Natural language processing models - Translation, Speech to text, smart assistant etc.
- Computer vision models - Object detection etc.
- Time series models - Financial markets, biological signals (visualize brain waves).

> What is inference? - Inference is the process of making predictions or estimations with a trained model. The inference latency is the time it takes a trained model to process a single input, with predicted output. This latency metric is important when using deep learning models to serve critical business applications in production environments where performant scalability and efficiency are key to both controlling costs and meeting customer demands for fast online services. Model latency has a number of structural factors which can be optimized to achieve a required inference performance:
>
> - The selected matrix operation for each layer which determines the number of operations that must be performed to complete a single discrete staged inference.
> - The size of the model and the data considered in an inference, which determines the amount of data that must be loaded and operated on between each discrete stage of an inference.

- It is not uncommon to see variations in performance differences between levels of quantization applied to compressed and uncompressed neural networks, for example, a compressed model from 32-bit to 16-bits can offer a double increase in operations per second.
- Several deep learning frameworks provide support for Quantization (-Aware Training), for example, PyTorch, ONNX, Glow.

As quantization obtains latency reductions by using less precision and increasing the number of computations per cycle, this also has the side effect of reducing the accuracy of the model.
