Today, most ML workloads executed at inference time are entirely sequential due to the thread-per-request model implemented in most inference engines (Client servers). Dynamic batching aims to change that by allowing for batch-parallel processing. Can we get some form of parallelism as we build up the data? In essence, dynamic batching reduces completion time by computing multiple requests in batch on a single device. Batching happens by taking data that might come in serially in a certain processor ordering and putting it into a packed format that allows ML frameworks to build up a batch that gets sent in. This technique is very useful for a lot of pervasive use cases, for example, sending images to a detector-segmentor model or audio data to a transducer model.
