Perplexity is a score that quantifies the performance of a supervised learning model. It is determined by applying the mode on the training set and using the result of each independent component of the training set to divide it’s true class by the prediction of the class.

One way to think about perplexity is that it is the probabilistic equivalent to accuracy or word error rate on a dataset. Perplexity is often used in natural language processing to quantify how well a model predicts a sample. The idea is to stimulate original text (sample) and then compute perplexity where the smaller the perplexity, the better it predicts a sample. For example, if you predict a very small sentence, the model should have a small perplexity (since it will probably not have any errors), but if you predict a big complicated article, it is going to have a big perplexity as it has more chances of making a mistake.

Perplexity can be likened to cross entropy. If `Ai` are true class labels and `Bi` are prediction probabilities, then:

```
perplexity = exp(cross_entropy)
```

For example, a perplexity of 100 means that your model is as confused about a sample as if you would randomly pick one word out of every 100 words to generate the text.

On the other hand, a perplexity of 1 means you knew which word to put, so your panelty is 0. So, we may better express the equation as:

```
perplexity = 2ˆ(cross_entropy)
```

As such, the perplexity for a calculation will be anywhere between 1-100 (though numbers of larger magnitude are possible in very complex calculations).

Generally, the lower the perplexity, the better. Human generated data has a perplexity of around 8 to 12.

If the model made a score of 2, that means your model was 50 confident that a given string is valid, than random chance and chose twice the number of samples, so in this case 100% of times as twice the number of samples is 2.

