Mixed precision training is a technique that uses both 32-bit precision (“float”) and 16-bit precision (“half”) numbers to train models. Most operations in deep learning models are matrix multiplications that are compute- and memory-bandwidth bound. Using 16-bit precision numbers instead of 32-bit numbers for such operations can give up to 2-3x faster training throughput at the same accuracy as 32-bit training. Mixed precision training requires reducing precision from 32 to 16 bits to train deep learning models at the same or at better accuracy than 32-bit training. NVIDIA provides Automatic Mixed Precision training functionality through PyTorch AMP APIs and the TensorFlow AMP optimizer, which allows users to do this in three easy steps.

1. Wrap model and input data in an `amp.autocast()` context.
