Often machine learning practitioners focus on building systems, using possibly performance-driven metrics, creating a snake that eats its own tail - taking wrong steps that lead to bad models.

Evaluation is an important part of an ML process, helping you to follow the right path. As the system grows in complexity, it's harder and harder to discover subtle bugs in the system, while it's more important to the product outcome.

Creating a solid evaluation strategy gives a clear picture of where the system is heading. So, here we are going to discuss
* evaluation strategies and success metrics
* whether you need to collect data and create tools for evaluation - and how to approach those processes.

It's usually ideal to dedicate an entire team to evaluation, as it is so important.


## What metrics to capture
The story of the paper has three stars: training data, model, and metrics. "Metrology" is the science of measuring, it is also important to measure those three stars. Sadly, our brains have many bugs making these tasks difficult.

Slowly, as you iterate, practicing engineering effort. When everything seems to work, you can fix your engineering process to extract the most value from your bootstrap round.

Clearly, you should  collect metrics AFTER the model has been trained and deployed.

### Ground truth labeling
Some metrics are important to track for creating general models (detector, models).

But some artifacts (systems, products) require you to obtain new data based on validation of a newly trained model.

Production experts (subject-matter experts, end-customers) will receive value - ie. provied high-added-value service - when new quality training data can be collected for recently deployed model via continuous evaluation. The artifact consumers can participate into a labeling process and receive reward.

Case: VisuNLP. Here, a clustering model might need metrics calculated on human evaluated clusters, even in production.

## Challenges
Once the model is deployed, that opens up a whole swarm of challenges.

### Sample persistence
Often introducing a model to a userbase changes user behavior, which can make a model's future performance not representative.

If an AI generates responses that are intelligent and similar to human speech, people will want to converse with that AI more. This can increase the usage of that product relative to when people were speaking to humans. The users may also gain familiarity with this technology and soften to it – changing how they respond, either in how much time and effort they spend writing to the chatbot, or in their belief they are speaking with another person.

### Labile domain
Next to that, your data can become stale.

## Common evaluation pitfalls

### Variance in evaluation metrics
Once your aggregate data, then you need high-quality metrics to draw a picture of the system. Which metrics to choose is not obvious too.

### Under-powered evaluation
