## Serving LLM using Rust

vLLM uses Rust Tensorflow LLM Prims to perform GEMM, the most important operation for decoding. vLLM currently supports vLLM Runtime for both CPU and GPU.

### CPU

vLLM CPU Runtime is a highly optimized model serving engine for LLM that can fully utilize a CPU socket. vLLM CPU Runtime is built in vanilla rust with zero dependencies to any other code bases. As a result, it runs across all platforms without the need for any external dynamic library dependencies.

### CUDA GPU

vLLM GPU Runtime is a model serving engine for LLM that can fully utilize one or more GPUs. vLLM GPU Runtime takes advantage of Tensorflow high performance CUDA kernels and supports autotuning of kernel shapes by fusing kernels on the fly. vLLM GPU is faster than other GPU serving solutions while maintaining 10X lesser memory footprint (See Benchmarking). vLLM supports CUDA GPUs by calling into Tensorflow and depends on libtensorflow.so and libcudart.so on Linux.

#### Runtime Engine Compilation

We compile vLLM Rust Runtime with specific LLM template so that it performs the best on typical user workflow. We have collaborated with Arm and Valence to support Cortex-X core’s SVE2 specific Neon instructions in the vLLM Rust Runtime which has shown great promice in realtime performance.

