#### Making elasticsearch feel more interactive

In a typical search application, a user types keywords and within a fraction of a second, a results page is displayed. This quick response time is intuitive and essential when utilizing a search engine. Think of a search engine as you would a calculator. If you want to calculate 1 byte in bits, you type in the command and expect to see a result nearly instantaneously. It’s the same with search.

However, if your search engine has a large index, the search performance can suffer, and the user experiences sluggish response times. If this happens, you need to consider implementing search latency optimization so that users have a more responsive experience.

Let’s look at how to optimize search latency and what steps you can take to ensure a speedy response time, because another way of viewing search latency is to think of it as the time it takes for the user to see her search results upon submitting information.

As developers, keeping search latency low and providing interactive performance is essential and impacts the entire product experience.

### So how can elasticsearch keep the latency low?

The answer is doc values and token count filtering optimization. These two features solve two different, yet intertwined, challenges for large-volume indexes. Let’s learn about doc values first.

##### Doc values

Field data is the name given to operations that require access to all possible values of a field in order to do their job. Examples of field data operations are sorting, aggregations, and suggesters. Because field data requires access to all values of a field, these operations can take up quite a bit of memory.

For example, let’s pretend that you want to aggregate by zip code. If there were 10,000 documents with that field, it means that Elasticsearch has to process 10,000 terms altogether. Adding to the problem, if the value of that field is high, for example, that can mean potentially more memory consumption.

However, doc values don’t require all values of a field to be accessed at once. Furthermore, the memory size of a single indexed value is small and fixed. It can potentially make the entire field fit comfortably in available memory (RAM).

However, one thing to watch out for is that when using doc values, your “search” latency will increase because it will need to retrieve the doc values each time from the disk. But the upside is that due to the lower memory requirement, your aggregations and faceting will run faster. In the end though, depending on your application. you may need to increase the available memory in order for Elasticsearch to process the aggregations quickly.

