## What is a seque-to-sequence model?

We use Neural Machine Translation (NMT) models to predict a complex output sequence from a given input sequence. In NMT, we input some text in a given language and output a corresponding translation in a different language. Given an input English sentence, we can produce the correct German translation. We can refer to the NMT model as a sequence-to-sequence model that takes a sequence as input and produces a sequence as output.

‍

## Sequence-to-sequence Background

NMT models share a common architectural component. Given an input sequence, an encoder network learns how to encode the sequence. The output of the encoder network passes to a decoder network, which learns to generate the translation. It isn’t evident how to apply this model because we cannot feed in the complete phrase into the NMT model and expect it to generate the correct result. Instead, we feed the entire source sequence into the NMT model one word at a time (some unspecified order), expecting it to generate the correct translated word.

However, this reveals a new challenge. What should the NMT model do after it outputs the respective word? What should be done for the rest of the input? Should it consider the outputted word for then next input? What about for rest of the source? How can we determine what the next input word should be?

The simplest strategy is, as above, to just consider the source ordering. Let’s look at German as the source language and English as the translation target. For the German sentence, “Die Liste der Nobelpreisträger für Literatur besteht aus 110 Personen, darunter 16 Frauen.”, we provide the complete English translation, “The list of Nobel laureates in literature consists of 110 people, 16 of whom are women.” as the input (shown below). This way, the only ordering of source and target words is the same.

Although, that isn’t particularly good for human reasoning. Sometimes, people don’t translate the words in order but may shuffle the positioning of words to make it grammatically correct. So this method restricts the German phrases to always be identical to the German sentences. It is not a genuine mechanism for learning a translation since it is more like dictation. So, the best approach is to train the NMT model to incorporate all of the outputs.

