## Quantization

Quantization is the process of reducing the precision range of a given signal to a discrete value. It is an integral part of all DSP and machine learning applications. You may have heard quantization being used in the context of image encoding using codecs such as H.26x, JPEG or MPEG using 8-bit quantization. When we talk about quantizing a word vector, we reduce it from a 32-bit floating-point precision signal to an 8-bit integer precision signal. To better understand the difference between floating-point precision and integer precision, check out this comparison of FP16, FP32, and INT8 precision on pyxtrememl.

### Performance Impacts

A bump from INT8 to FP32 precision can lead to huge memory footprints of your workload, causing more significant latency on inference operations and reducing inference throughput.

But that’s just talking about what’s in memory and being utilized on the GPU. There’s the matter of how long your operation takes to load to the GPU. When working with models in inference on GPUs, floating-point precision has the advantage of drawing from the large array of GPU cache readily available. In contrast, low-precision signals like INT8 workloads require loading much of this memory from the main CPU to perform computations. This loading causes a significant reduction in inference throughput.

It’s important to note that the accuracy differences between the floating-point and integer precipitation workloads for transformer models aren’t that great. But their throughput differences are sizable.

To demonstrate this, I trained a BERT-base uncased model using Hugging Face’s Tutorials. Using GitHub repository fnl/nlp_sentiment_analysis, I loaded in a 101k Twitter dataset to classify posts as negative or positive.

Let’s plot our accuracy and throughputs:

Having a significantly larger latency for an operation (shown above on the FP16 scale, 0:04 second vs. 30 seconds) and not having a significant accuracy benefit doesn’t seem to be a good trade.

But what if there were some sort of compromise between FP32/FP16 accuracy and inference performance, and INT8 accuracy and inference performance?

### Can we have the best of both worlds with transformers?

There is value in quantizing word vectors. Quantization using dynamic quantization allows for reduction in GPU memory requirement. It helps overlap gpu and cpu memory, and when set up efficiently, to get about ~3x latency compared to FP16 while keeping the model’s accuracy within ~5% of FP16.

## Why use Dynamic Quantization?

1. Dynamic quantization achieves the best of both worlds in terms of inference throughput and model accuracy. By going into 16-bit precision and loading weights as INT8, we keep around 95% of the model accuracy while having close to 3x the throughput of FP16 inference throughput.
2. Any necessary retraining of models using NeMo can be completely automated (no extra labeling required!)
3. Running inference on models dynamically quantized with FP16 is easier than running inference on 8-bit models due to ease of floating-point convergence. Staying in your current floating-point models, and thus leaving your training infrastructure untouched is a huge plus.
4. Dynamic quantization avoids the bottleneck of latency in the total GPU memory by swiftly swapping data from CPU to GPU memory (called memory swap).

