Packet-wise parallelism means that processing of data (token) is concurrently performed by different processing elements that process different parts of the same data (token) and that may communicate using different types of interconnect. Since a different processing element executes a different stage of processing of the token, this is a kind of pipelined parallelism.

A communication segment in a Token Ring topology-based network may be configured such that it has a single logical channel that has a data/control rate of, e.g., 4 or 16 Mbps. This configuration reduces the hardware complexity and can afford an increase of the network efficiency by token-wise parallelism. This kind of parallelism doesn't make sense in a bus topology since the token does not need to be pumped through the network and it's straightforward to achieve packet-wise parallelism.

In the token-ring topology, for 4 Mbps, you will have a serial (ring) interconnect. For 16 Mbps, you will want to go to a parallel interconnect since after a certain threshold, it is not cost-effective to increase the speed on a given technology. However, it is much more economical and cost-effective to increase the parallelism or connectivity.

- A token (control signal) is transmitted on a uni or bi-directional-ring composed of serial or parallel ports of the nodes and interconnects using specific protocols. In addition, it is a single- or multiring (multiple processes on the same node or ring). Thus, packets can be transmitted concurrently on different physical rings as part of the token-ring network (regardless of whether all the rings are in sync or not). In addition, since the ring topology is pseudo-random with a particle traversing (adding or removing content from) the different processes and nodes in its path, it is also possible to concatenate tokens (packets or transactions) into superpackets (which can consist of independent packets from different transactions), which can serialize/deserialize and break apart/put back together the superpackets at the appropriate time and place. This data-segmentation could alleviate any thundering herds of transitions of multiple transactions accessing the same shared data by first serializing the multiple transactions (that access the same shared data) into a superpacket (token) and, thus, perform one serialize/deserialize action to a set of shared data.
- There are many interesting implementation options:
    - Single ring with active ports on certain nodes for communication and processing.
    - Single ring with passive ports on all the nodes for receiving communications and only a certain number of ports physically active for processing communications. (Multiple spurs/rings at the same time from same machine with independent processing of multiple rings.)
    - Single ring with preprocessing to identify/authenticate the nodes and route communications.
    - Single- or multiring with token (signal) combining devices (splitting token communication or serializing and spreading out/re-serializing the single token (and the data) into multiple tokens in a superpacket (maintaining the sequence and/or ordering). The tokens could have different types (or priorities of execution based on types).

