Hello Nameda!In this exercise, we are asked to describe the difference between loss and perplexity…Loss is a penalty function for differences in probabilities between gold and predicted labels. If those probabilities are close to each other, the loss is lower. Given a gold (aka “true”) label and a probability distribution over labels, the loss is:
loss = -log(p(true_label))
Perplexity values an entire model, and it is probably the most popular way to report experimental results in your papers. To compute the perplexity, we sum up the loss for all sentences ∑‎loss_i and divide it by the number of sentences n. Following that, we compute:

```
perplexity = exp(∑‎loss_i/n)
```

The smaller the perplexity, the better the language model. Mathematically, it is an exponent of average negative log-likelihood of the test data.By the way, our example has 0 as perplexity since it has zero loss. Hope that helps!Best,The 365 Team
