A cold start happens when a machine is called into action after a period of inactivity. Starting a car for the first time for the day after a night of inactivity is a cold start. A sleepy Monday morning rally is a cold start. The first run after vacation; a cold start.

The first morning you hit the streets in your early Winter purchase, the engine will sputter and strain to fire. It’ll ease when it gets up to speed. A cold start.

The same logic holds with scaleable machine learning model serving, where the scale moves with the volume of your model inferences. Both Google and AWS, for example, have local GPU containers for cache-friendly model inference at low latency. But when the model usage spikes, that capacity for inference spreads to other machines in the cloud – and, suddenly the model and framework in which it sits are not natively installed on the machine. The model hasn’t run on the machine recently and is deemed to be “cold”. Upon arriving at that machine for the first time, the machine will require time to load the model (which will depend on its size), install the server dependencies for the framework of choice, the inference container or Docker(opens in a new tab) image of choice, and configure server environments and settings.

This first run in the new machine and/or serverless environment is, of course, a cold start – and, as you’d anticipate, not the optimal serving experience. Once you’ve past the cold start, things can speed up.

## So what makes a cold start?

A cold start will vary depending on several aspects of your serverless environment. Your model size, configuration, and deployment and server environment are the major factors. There will also be variation in individual deployment types, as there will be differences between cold start generation and the nature of the cold start across hosting providers.

Do we care about cold starts or is it unavoidable?

Absolutely. Customers care about latency in machine learning – and in web services(opens in a new tab) more broadly. Cold starts in model inference can mean web service critical latency of several seconds, depending on machine size and inference performance. That could spell the difference between a great user experience and a delayed one. SageMaker’s own README(opens in a new tab) reports that initial cold-start latency may be up to several seconds at times due to container provisioning. It’s clear that optimizations are needed.

Avoiding a cold start in model inference deployment

