Sameer Qureshi(opens in a new tab), a senior product manager at AWS, points to the huge variations in cold-start times across frameworks in a blog on Lambda and EKS endpoints optimization.

Qureshi notes that in one experiment, the choice of framework played a huge difference in latency on cold start. His proposed solutions don’t quite work for deep learning model serving; nevertheless, they’re indicative. In one experiment, Scala cold start on Lambda was 18 milliseconds to 22 milliseconds compared to the 53 milliseconds to 72 milliseconds for Python.

Python is host to the most popular deep learning frameworks(opens in a new tab), and the Edge has put in a concerted effort to boot up Python fast and furious with projects like pyperformance(opens in a new tab) (reaching parity in 2018) or Pyston(opens in a new tab), now at Dropbox(opens in a new tab) . Milksop(opens in a new tab), an experimental Python server built for fast startup using the Rust compiler, stands out for its attempts to boost startup times. Provisioning compute in JavaScript, which is essential for the browser, is simply not the intention of AWS’ server instance model, so it’d be a curious adventure down that path.

## Auto scaling?

Auto scaling in deployment is one solution for inhibiting the impact of a single cold start. Co-developing AI and IoT products on SageMaker, Amazon’s AtariANt set of machine learning models, allows models to scale deferentially to meet concurrent demands.

