 This post covers the basics of how GraphQl allows slower queries to render their data for faster-completed queries before any content is displayed on the screen.

In essence, this is possible due to fine-grained per-query timeouts managed by operations. Each batch operation has a timer that determines when the timeout is hit. If the operation never hits this time limit, you can have an asynchronous data-fetching operation happen on your server (e.g., while you’re loading other things from the database).

An asynchronous data fetcher might want to push this timeout out beyond what it originally set so that it can wait on some expensive data before sending its response back to the client. If we ever reach our timeout limit and no other batch operations have timed out yet, then we’ll stop processing batches until either more than one element is complete or everything fails out due to a user timeout.

How ever, we do not want to interrupt the execution of our data fetchers while they are in progress and be forced to restart them. This would slow down an already slow user experience, which is why we push it out beyond what we originally set for the timeout interval.

After the initial passing through all of these batches, there will be a waterfall effect that occurs when multiple batches pass through at once after each one has completed on its own time limit (or failed out due to some other reason). A problem occurs with this approach when it comes to batch sizes that are larger than an individual operation.

In other words, if we have a large batch size and someone requests information from a subset of its slow-functioning operations, the timing on when those requests are submitted to the server should be averaged out.

However, in this case, things could go wrong! We do not want to send all those small-batch requests at once because that would lead to wasted bandwidth when the server’s response finally arrives. To avoid wasting bandwidth with no benefit, we start sending small batches immediately after an individual operation is complete so they arrive no faster than what it took (the original query) to finish running on its own time limits (or faster) back on the client! This can be important if operations have timeouts that depend on how many items are in the batch.
