The colors are 0 for black as the base, and 1 – 9 for dark to light gray in ascending order.

The Red Square highlights some of the pixels in the image.

A Convolution Layer has a group of neurons that are each responsible for only a couple of pixels of the input image.

Each neuron is connected to a set of pixels.

Each connection has a certain strength.

In this case, multiple neurons will be processing the 5 by 5 input image, each responsible for some of the pixels.

These multiple neurons form a so-called feature map.

The Feature Map will then be sent for subsampling (discussed next).

Note that in practice, more than one Feature Map can exist and they can be stacked on top of each other.

Also, there may be more than one convolution layer.

Multiple convolution layers learn to output and detect increasingly abstracted (higher level) features.

For example, a convolution layer can output an intermediate feature, which could be a vertical or horizontal line, which are two such visual patterns.

Later convolution layers can build on that feature to output slightly higher-level features, such as a corner piece.

## What is a SubSampling Layer?

A SubSampling Layer takes the output from the convolution layer and converts it into decreased sampled images requiring less computational power.

The output is less computation than the input because the dimensionality has been reduced.

Subsampling improves the efficiency by reducing the number of parameters the model needs to learn.

So SubSampling Layers perform downsampling so that the volume size of the input data is reduced, while conserving the extracted and relevant features.

